workbook :ide_workbench, type: :design

# The Code workbench — our own custom Svelte IDE, on one Dock seam, VS-Code-compatible by adapter

We tried embedding the VS Code workbench (monaco-vscode-api) and forking Theia. Both are **dead strategies**
— ripped out. The embed loaded slowly, lost its CSS on navigation, shipped the wrong icons, and never read as
ours. The whole point is a surface that *is* ours. So the Code section is a fully-custom Svelte composition
over the **Files** primitives (the file tree + the CodeMirror editor, themed from `app.css`) wrapped in our own
chrome — floating workbench toolbar, collapsible terminal panel, status strip. No monaco, no `@codingame/*`.

## The Dock — one capability seam

Everything the workbench does goes through **`dock.*`** (`src/lib/dock/`), the single host surface from the
platform's Dock/`invoke` canon. A **provider** fulfills each call:

- `local` (today) — answers in-browser over the mock tree (`fs.svelte.js`) + pure-JS stand-ins.
- `runtime` (later) — RCP/WS to the nexus: `fs`→WASHIE (Nexus.Store SQLite), `shell`→Nexus.Shell (the shell
  compiled to one wasm module), `lang`→a wasm LSP server. **Swapping providers is config, not a rewrite.**

Surface: `dock.fs` · `dock.shell` · `dock.lang` · `dock.build` · `dock.work` · `dock.vcs` · `dock.ext`.
`WorkTerminal` is a thin view over `dock.shell.exec`; the editor's language features call `dock.lang.*`.

## VS Code extensions — consumed in layers, never by re-embedding VS Code

The valuable parts of "extensions" are decoupled **protocols and data**, not the VS Code app:

1. **LSP (language intelligence) — DONE (local impl).** The editor's completion · diagnostics · hover come
   from `dock.lang.*` (CodeMirror `autocompletion`/`linter`/`lintGutter`/`hoverTooltip`, themed to our tokens).
   The local provider gives real `.work` intelligence (kind/keyword/symbol completion, do/end-balance + unknown-
   kind diagnostics, hover docs). A wasm LSP server fulfills the SAME `dock.lang` calls later — no editor change.
2. **Open VSX registry — DONE (live).** `Extensions.svelte` browses **open-vsx.org** (the vendor-neutral
   marketplace; MS's own forbids third-party clients) via `dock.ext.search/get` — real public REST: search,
   icons, versions, downloads, Install. Toggled into the left column from the toolbar (activity-bar model, no
   2nd rail).
3. **TextMate grammars + VS Code color themes** — pure JSON/plist data, consumable directly (Shiki/
   `codemirror-textmate`). Not default-on: our brand theme wins; importing a foreign theme is a capability, not
   the default. (Foundation in place via the data path; not yet surfaced in UI.)
4. **Full VSIX extension code — seam DONE, runtime later.** A VSIX is untrusted JS (`extension.js` activating
   against the `vscode` namespace). The thesis path: **compile it to wasm, run it in the sandbox** against a
   `vscode` **shim mapped onto the Dock** (`src/lib/dock/ext-host.js`): `workspace.fs`→`dock.fs` ·
   `window.createTerminal`→`dock.shell` · `languages.registerCompletionItemProvider`→our provider registry ·
   `commands.registerCommand`→our command map. A **built-in sample extension** (`workbooks.snippets`), authored
   exactly like a real VSIX and knowing nothing of the Dock, activates against the shim and contributes snippet
   completions that flow into the editor — proving the whole chain (vscode API → shim → Dock → editor) end-to-
   end today. A downloaded VSIX runs identically, just compiled-to-wasm in the sandbox (the washy 70%).

## State

Layers 1, 2, 4 are live and verified in the dash; layer 3 has its data path but no UI yet. Loads ~400ms,
survives navigation. The Dock is both **our API** and the **VS-Code-compat layer**, so "make it work per the
API we'd want" and "integrate VS Code extensions" are the same seam, approached once.

## What is REAL vs a SEAM (anti-decoration audit)

Every control traces to a consumer — no decorative buttons:
- **Real, working:** Explorer/Extensions toggles · Toggle Terminal · the terminal (input + Run + Weave all run
  through ONE shared scrollback `term.svelte.js` → `dock.shell.exec`: `ls/tree/cat/run/weave/ext/help`) ·
  editor completion/diagnostics/hover (`dock.lang`) · Open VSX search (`dock.ext.search`) · status-bar branch
  (`dock.vcs.status`).
- **Honest seam (intentionally not "done" — wired later, labelled as such):** Extensions **Install** (the
  VSIX→wasm host) · the ext-host `vscode` shim's `workspace.fs` / `window` / `commands` branches (the compat
  CONTRACT a real VSIX needs, even though the built-in sample only uses completion) · `dock.fs.*` (used by the
  shim + the future runtime provider; the Svelte UI binds the reactive store `fs.svelte.js`, which IS the local
  provider's backing — same data, not a second path).
- **Cut as speculative (had zero consumers):** `dock.build.weave` · `dock.work.parse` · `dock.fs.stat` ·
  `dock.lang.format` · `dock.ext.get` · the `workbooks.hello` sample command · the dead Run/Source-control
  toolbar buttons · the fake Output/Problems terminal tabs. Re-add each only when a real consumer exists.
